  "",
  "Penn ADAPT builds assistive and adaptive technology. I co-lead its prosthetic arm project, an 8-channel surface EMG acquisition and actuation system designed to turn muscle signals into motion. I wrote the acquisition firmware and contributed to the schematic and layout of the sensor modules in Altium. EMG signals have driven the arm. The STM32H730 integration and the move to two servos are not finished.",
  "- The sensing side is designed as eight modules, each with a 24-bit ADS1291, on one 10 MHz SPI bus. Each module has its own chip-select line, and the START pins are tied so every channel samples together. One shared bus replaced eight microcontroller peripherals and simplified routing.",
  "- The main board's STM32H730 is a Cortex-M7, chosen for headroom to run on-board TinyML gesture classification later. The board has a DRV8874 H-bridge for the linear actuator and PWM outputs for a dual-servo joint stage. A 2S LiPo feeds 12 V, 6 V and 3.3 V rails. The 3.3 V sensing rail is kept separate from the actuator supply.",
  "- I implemented the PWM and control interfaces, with current sensing and fault feedback from the H-bridge. I also built a modular Raspberry Pi Pico interface for actuator prototyping and per-user calibration.",
  "- I used a 4-layer stackup with dedicated ground and power planes. During sensor bring-up I traced a repeating artifact to SPI clock coupling into the analog front end. I rerouted the sensitive traces and the artifact disappeared.",
  "- Project page: /projects/adapt-emg-board.html",
  "",
  "### Penn IEEE (Penn IEEE student branch): President and Co-Chair, Jan 2026 to now",
  "",
  "Penn IEEE is the University of Pennsylvania's student branch of IEEE. It connects about 180 members across electrical, computer and systems engineering with industry and faculty. I have been president and co-chair since January 2026, and I lead a board of five officers.",
  "- I organized a fireside chat with Dr. Nader Engheta, the H. Nedwill Ramsey Professor at Penn. He is known for his work on metamaterials and optical computing, and about 50 people attended.",
  "- I organized and co-moderated a joint alumni panel with Penn Architechs on career paths in hardware and software. Nine panelists from NVIDIA, Apple, CrowdStrike, Lutron and other companies also covered recruiting and graduate school decisions.",
  "- I helped draft new constitution articles that formalize officer roles, elections and succession, so the branch runs consistently year to year. I also prepared our materials for the IEEE Student Activities Conference.",
  "- We run recurring technical workshops on topics like PCB design, embedded systems and FPGAs, plus industry info sessions and company tech talks. Social and mentorship events pair underclassmen with upperclassmen, and past events include a Penn Engineering alumni social.",
  "- Active membership has grown from 100 to 180, and we secured $1,000 in sponsorship and university funding. We have hosted six events so far in the 2026 to 2027 academic year.",
  "",
  "### PennAiR (Penn Aerial Robotics): Electrical Hardware Engineer, Sep 2025 to Jan 2026",
  "",
  "At Penn Aerial Robotics I was one of six students who designed a custom STM32-based flight controller in Altium Designer for the team's competition UAVs. The goal was lower cost and weight than a commercial Pixhawk. I was on the team from September 2025 to January 2026.",
  "- The targets against a commercial Pixhawk were a cost reduction of more than 50% and a weight reduction of 30%.",
  "- I designed the power management, which converts USB or battery input to 5 V, 3.3 V and 1.8 V rails.",
  "- I also designed the two memory modules for flight data logging and the power sensing. The board was fabricated.",
  "- Project page: /projects/pennair-flight-controller.html",
  "",
